tput, so this is from reading it. I'm assuming Postgres.\n\n**Scans the query forces**\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** `lower(u.email) = '...'` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The database reads every user row and lowercases each email.\n2. **Full scan of `orders`, probably.** The join runs on `o.user_id`. Postgres doesn't index foreign keys automatically. Without an index on `orders.user_id`, it reads the whole table, or hash-joins against all of it, to find one user's orders. This is the most likely cause of eight seconds, because `orders` is the big table.\n3. **Wide row fetch from `SELECT *`.** This pulls every column from both tables. It rules out index-only scans and drags large columns (JSON, text blobs) through the join.\n\n**Fixes**\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Make the email lookup indexable (add UNIQUE if emails are unique)\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- 2. Make the join side indexable\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n-- If the page sorts by recency, use this instead:\n-- CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_created ON orders (user_id, created_at DESC);\n```\n\n```sql\n-- 3. Select only what the page renders\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.status, o.total\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\nThe expression in the query has to match the index exactly (`lower(email)`). A longer-term option is to store emails already lowercased, or use `citext`. Then you could query `email = ...` with an ordinary index.\n\n**Why it got slow \"since last week\"**\n\nAn index that used to exist may have been dropped. A migration may have changed the column type or the `lower()` wrapper. The table may also have crossed a size threshold where the planner switched plans. Run this on the real database to check:\n\n```sql\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) <the query>;\n```\n\nLook for `Seq Scan on orders` or `Seq Scan on users`. If the plan shows something else, such as an index scan that is still slow, send it to me. The bottleneck would be elsewhere, for example in the application layer or in a page that runs this query once per row.\n\nIf you can share the schema or the `EXPLAIN` output, I can confirm which of these is the real culprit.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}